{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Research melt temperature parameters: foundry pouring temps for aluminum bronzes, gas atomization temps for NAB, and published ultrasonic atomization of NAB. Assess superheat effects.",
  "rationale": "Question 1 requires comparison of 1300°C with standard practice and published literature for C63000/similar alloys.",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": "Found the key paper (Uhlmann 2024, doi:10.3390/ma17143396) on ultrasonic atomization of NAB. Need to read it in detail and find more data on foundry pouring temps and gas atomization temps.",
  "evaluation": null
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Research melt temperature parameters for C63000 ultrasonic atomization",
  "rationale": "Needed to assess whether 1300°C is appropriate",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Uhlmann 2024 confirms 1300°C was used for ultrasonic atomization of CuAl10Ni5Fe5 on the AMAZEMET AUS 500. C63000 liquidus ~1054°C gives ~246°C superheat at 1300°C. Foundry practice for NAB typically 1150-1200°C (100-150°C superheat). Gas-atomized NAB powder (Krebs 2016) used electrode induction melting. The 1300°C is the atomizer's maximum and matches published NAB atomization.",
  "evaluation": "Found published precedent at 1300°C and foundry comparisons. Sufficient for analysis."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Research nozzle clogging mechanisms for Al-bronze melts",
  "rationale": "Need to explain why turbo pressure was needed continuously",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Al2O3 skin formation on Al-bronze is well-known in foundry practice. Surface tension of liquid Cu-10Al is ~1.35 N/m (Schmitz 2014). Capillary back-pressure in 0.5mm bore is significant. Combined with oxide blockage and potential freezing, these explain the flow difficulty. Steel casting alumina clogging literature (Aksel'rod 2007) provides analogies.",
  "evaluation": "Can provide quantitative capillary pressure estimates and multiple mechanism assessment."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Research thermodynamic compatibility of C63000 melt with BN, graphite, alumina",
  "rationale": "Need to assess crucible and rod material suitability",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Williams 1984 provides thermodynamic framework for Al activity in Cu alloys. Al activity in Cu-10Al is significant (~0.01-0.05 at 1300°C). Al+BN→AlN+B reaction is thermodynamically marginal. Al+C→Al4C3 is thermodynamically favorable. Carbon pickup from graphite by Fe/Ni in Cu is expected. Carbon fibers from sonotrode found in UA powder (Uhlmann 2024).",
  "evaluation": "Have thermodynamic framework and experimental evidence of contamination."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Research W and Mo solubility in Cu-Al-Ni-Fe melts",
  "rationale": "Need to assess plate dissolution risk",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "W solubility in Cu is very low (<0.01 wt% at 1300°C). Al-W intermetallics exist but require high Al activities. Lower Ni in C63000 vs Cu-30Ni reduces W solubility risk. Al2O3 skin on NAB melt may reduce wetting of W plate. Carbon fiber substrate adds carbon pickup risk.",
  "evaluation": "Can provide estimates based on binary W-Cu solubility data and Al effects."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Research flake formation mechanisms in ultrasonic atomization",
  "rationale": "Need to explain flake/splat presence in powder",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Yang 2026 describes how film thickness, oxide skins, and amplitude affect atomization quality. Uhlmann 2024 shows spatters in UA powder. Excessive melt flow rate (from turbo pressure) overwhelms atomization capacity. Al2O3 skins prevent spheroidization.",
  "evaluation": "Have mechanism evidence from multiple sources."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Research AM powder requirements for NAB",
  "rationale": "Need composition, oxygen, and microstructure requirements",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Uhlmann 2024 UA NAB oxygen = 0.019% (190 ppm). Gas-atomized NAB = 350-410 ppm (Krebs 2016). LPBF NAB achieves >99.9% density. As-built microstructure: martensitic β'/β with suppressed κ phases. Heat treatment 675°C/6h per ASTM B505 needed for corrosion resistance. C63000 requires Cu + named elements ≥99.5%.",
  "evaluation": "Have oxygen data, microstructure info, and composition standards."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 7, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 7,
  "objective": "Research effects of oversized charge",
  "rationale": "Need to assess 1.08 kg vs 250-500 g guidance",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "[name redacted] 2023 describes the AMAZEMET rePOWDER system. Larger charge increases melt time, thermal non-uniformity, sonotrode heat load, and coating wear. 2-4x recommended charge size is significant.",
  "evaluation": "Can provide assessment based on general principles and published system capabilities."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 8, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 8,
  "objective": "Research safety considerations",
  "rationale": "Need safety info for NAB powder handling",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Cashdollar 1994: pure Cu and Ni did not ignite in 20-L tests. However, copper alloy dust SDS classifies it as combustible dust and flammable solid. Ni content (4-5.5%) requires attention for inhalation. ACGIH TLV for Ni = 1.5 mg/m3 (inhalable).",
  "evaluation": "Have key safety data from literature and SDS."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 9, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 9,
  "objective": "Create summary table and Cu-30Ni vs C63000 comparison",
  "rationale": "Explicitly requested by user",
  "status": "in-progress",
